[Music]  had i made a video like this say  five or six years ago i'm fairly certain  that a large majority of fans of the  zelda series  including me would have answered with a  resounding no  no no but as of late i've seen more and  more people being open to the idea or  even flat out calling for nintendo to  break the tradition of link's silent  demeanor and give him a proper voice  i myself started to think about this  more when gerard khalil aka the  completionist addressed this in his  review for breath the wild back in 2017.  i will say and maybe a ton of people  will really disagree with me on this but  i think it's time to give link a voice  as well as angry joe's take on the  matter  and i get that he's supposed to be you  and you're supposed to project yourself  onto him but in my opinion it's 2017  and those are born from older game  limitations  and since then i too have slowly become  more open to the idea of nintendo giving  it a shot in future installments  now i'm not here to tell you whether or  not link should have a voice or not  i'm just here to give my two cents on it  and no matter which direction nintendo  decides to take it i will always remain  a loyal fan of the series either way  so if you disagree that is totally fine  and i actually encourage a discussion on  the matter because  at the end of the day we all want what's  best for the series right  so what is it about a voiced link that  has people on the fence  and why is nintendo been reluctant to  give it a try even to this day  obviously in the early days it was  mostly due to technical limitations  primitive sound chips and limited  storage capacity gave devs a hard time  incorporating good quality sound and  music into their games god knows they  tried though but it wasn't exactly the  most pleasant thing to listen to  but as technology advanced this became  less and less of a problem  still even to this day audio is one of  the biggest culprits when it comes to  the enormous file sizes of modern games  alongside high quality textures while  nintendo is known for its innovation  when it comes to gameplay experiences  both in terms of software and hardware  they have also lagged behind on the  competition on multiple occasions  and nowhere is this more clear than in  the mid to late 90s  the n64 cartridges could only hold up to  64 megabytes of data  which is less than your average music  album meanwhile sony fully embraced  cd-rom technology which could store  up to 660 megabytes more than 10 times  that of the n64 cartridges it is no  surprise then that the majority of games  featuring a fully voiced cast  pre-rendered cutscenes and non-midi  soundtracks are found here when it comes  to this generation of consoles  the pc obviously being in a league of  its own still even on the n64  some games manage to incorporate plenty  of voiced sections  games like perfect dark and conker's bad  fur day which also happened to be among  the biggest games on the system in terms  of size for obvious reasons  however ocarina of time only utilized  half of the storage capacity of its  cartridge so it's not like you could  make the argument that they weren't able  to at least have some spoken word in its  cut scenes  it's clearly something they either felt  was unnecessary or didn't fit the game  and  honestly i don't blame them the audio  quality for voices  wasn't exactly the greatest back then  due to the heavy compression needed to  make it fit on the cartridge  plus there wasn't really a high standard  for voice acting in video games yet like  there is now  if anything voice acting in ocarina of  time would have probably dated the game  a lot more  but gaming has come a long way since  then nowadays you could fit every n64  game ever made onto one single switch  cartridge which is kinda crazy to think  about but even when technology had  already caught up  nintendo was still hesitant to take it  to the next level even as late as 2006  nintendo was still resorting to mostly  midi tracks for the music in the legend  of zelda  twilight princess the same year that  oblivion made its debut which not only  featured a fully voiced cast  even for the most obscure npcs but also  a fully orchestrated soundtrack to go  with it but one thing that oblivion and  later skyrim and zelda have in common is  that the games feature a silent  protagonist  apart from the occasional grunts they  don't audibly speak neither during  gameplay or in cutscenes  silent protagonists have been around for  a long time and will likely always stick  around  and in oblivion the silent protagonist  approach made sense since you create  your own custom avatar to your own  liking  you can be a human an orc a furry mark  zuckerberg whatever floats your boat  a big part of role playing games like  these is making a lot of choices  you can be a lying stealing murdering  son of a bee or a goody two-shoes who  does  everything by the book or somewhere in  between this makes it very difficult to  present a cohesive cut-scene heavy  narrative  i mean it's kind of hard to be heralded  as a big hero the pinnacle of morality  and bravery when you just got done  murdering the local blacksmith because  he looked at you funny  but these issues don't matter for the  zelda series because the games have  never been  heavy on choices you play as a  pre-established hero set on a  predetermined path and with a specific  physique and skill  set and unlike games like fallout and  skyrim you don't get to choose whether  or not link is good at bartering or how  much charisma or intelligence he has  you don't get to murder innocent  civilians or step out of character all  of a sudden  no matter what kind of person you are  link will do what is required of him to  complete his adventure  sure you can waltz into a stranger's  house and smash up their pottery but  aside from that and a few small choices  here and there it has little to no  impact on the actual outcome every  cutscene will always play out the same  way  and the end goal 2 remains the same and  that is how zelda is meant to be in my  opinion  i don't want zelda to become like  oblivion where i can make link do the  most horrible things and yet still  somehow be heralded as a hero but that  doesn't mean that giving him a voice  ruins or changes that experience  in fact as early as zelda 2 it's already  been made clear that link does speak  and across the whole series this is  confirmed time and time again  characters who ask him his name and then  reiterate it based on his answer  every dialogue choice you make is him  saying that sentence causing the other  character to react accordingly heck we  even hear him speak in the wind waker  only two words but hey it's a start no  matter how shallow  some of his incarnations are every link  does in fact have a distinct personality  of their own  i mean can you honestly put wind waker  link skyward sword link and breath of  the wild link next to each other and  make the argument that they are all the  exact same person but with different  looks  heck no that is why when it comes to the  argument that link  needs to stay silent because he's the  vessel through which we experience the  adventure i have to say  sorry but i don't buy it i don't buy  this argument anymore  there are tons of examples of games  featuring a voice protagonist which  still  allow players to step into their shoes i  don't know about you but when i play  uncharted or horizon zero dawn  i find no issues projecting myself onto  nathan drake or alloy  i mean eloy i can still be that  character despite them having a  completely different  personality than that of my own after  all narrative driven video games are  often about stepping into someone else's  shoes and experiencing their journey  regardless of personality physique or  gender  in the last of us i am joel in tomb  raider i  am lara croft in tetris i am the tetris  block  needless to say not every game needs a  voiced protagonist when i play luigi's  mansion 3 or animal crossing i never  think to myself  man why can't these characters just have  normal conversations instead of this  weird semi-gibberish  i mean the games aren't exactly plot  heavy they're meant to be simple fun  adventures centered around gameplay and  while gameplay is equally important in  the zelda series  it is also one of nintendo's more  cinematic and story-driven ips and it's  clear that nintendo thinks the same way  given the fact that they recently  introduced voice acting it's clearly a  step that they felt was necessary to  take the series to the next level  and here's the thing breath of the wild  still kinda managed to get away with  having a silent link because it kept its  story to an absolute minimum  all the voiced sections are saved for  cut scenes only which are few and far  between  the rest of its story is told through  its environment and during gameplay it  still falls back to text-based  conversations  and you know what i'm totally fine with  that in fact i think there's a charm to  reading the dialogue and imagining the  conversation in your head  i've never been the biggest fan of the  modern day approach where you and the  npcs stare awkwardly at each other while  they exchange words  something always seemed a bit off about  having your character just stand there  dead-eyed while you choose your dialogue  option but that's just me  another problem with this approach is  that you're never going to be able to  have hundreds of voice actors for  every npc in the game which often  results in a lot of reused voices and  not every voice actor  is gonna be as good as the others it  doesn't ruin my experience of a game by  any means  but i just can't imagine something like  this in a zelda game i am more than  happy if they just stick the text based  conversations for the majority of the  gameplay  cutscenes is a different story however  as of right now we don't know what  breath of the wild sequel will do when  it comes to its narrative so it's hard  to say if a voiced link will even be  necessary in that game but if it's going  to take even the slightest bit of  inspiration from age of calamity then i  feel like nintendo might need to  consider giving it a try  because age of calamity is where the  first cracks in this silent approach  really started to rear its head this  game has a lot of cutscenes like  three hours worth of cutscenes and  here's my main point  the more people around link start  talking the more awkward it's going to  become to have him stay totally silent  in previous games everything was based  on dialog boxes in ad-libs which was  fine for the time and  it was consistent but now that close to  every character has started speaking  link stands out more and more as this  weird anomaly  okay i just realized how inconsiderate  and insulting this can sound to someone  who's  actually mute in real life so i want to  make it clear that i am by no means  saying that you are weird in fact you  are awesome  i'm just talking about video games here  like i said before link isn't really  mute  people ask him questions he answers the  only reason we don't hear him speak is  because nintendo simply refuses to do it  just wanted to make that clear anyway  breath of the wild broke a lot of  conventions and in some aspects it seems  like nintendo still had one foot in the  past  and didn't dare to go all the way with  it if nintendo plans to continue this  trend of having a fully voiced cast  i think it would be wise if they choose  a more consistent format  generally games have three different  approaches when it comes to dialogue  either everyone speaks everyone but the  protagonist speaks or nobody speaks and  it's all done through text  body language or gibberish and ad-libs  there are examples of games which do the  same thing as breath of the wild  they don't feature a wide array of  narrative choices and have a  pre-established main character yet still  choose to have a silent protagonist  while everyone else is voiced  half-life being a good example of this  now don't get me wrong the half-life  series is absolutely amazing  it is to this day in my top 10 favorite  franchises of all time  but even here the silent approach to the  main character gordon freeman is  starting to show its age if you ask me  especially since there isn't really a  point to him being silent anymore in  half-life 1 most npcs didn't contribute  much to the story other than to help set  the mood for the chaos and devastation  inside the laboratory besides this game  came out in like  1998 but in half-life 2 and even more so  half-life 2 chapter 1 large portions of  the game sees alex vance accompanying  you on your journey through city 17  and she talks quite a lot in fact she  has a very strong personality and is  actually one of my favorite companion  characters in any game to this day  at the time it didn't really bother me  that much but looking back at it now i  can  kinda see the awkwardness of having one  character talking your ears off  and the other well not talking at all  and similar to link it's not like gordon  is incapable of speech either  he is but for some reason we don't hear  it we're supposed to imagine it i guess  this creates this weird dynamic where  alex basically has to reiterate to the  player what it is that gordon might be  saying to her  and who knows it might even be the  reason why their latest game half-life  alex has you play as her  instead of gordon if what is being  speculated is true and we see link and  zelda work together a lot more in breath  of the wild too  it's unavoidable that we get the same  dynamic scene in half-life 2  where zelda has to act like link is  speaking or ignore the fact that he  isn't  and act as link's voice and has to  reiterate his imaginary dialogue to us  this would be fine of course but if the  goal is to see these two form a closer  connection  having links stay silent might get in  the way of that there would always be  this brick wall where link isn't able to  express his feelings and emotions  properly  now suppose nintendo indeed decides to  give link a voice  this of course doesn't mean he has to  all of a sudden be an open book and  rattle off dialogue after dialogue in  fact it was one of my gripes when i  recently played horizon zero dawn for  the first time  i love eloy as a character but this  chick talks to herself  way too much for my taste i know she's  supposed to be an outcast who's used to  being on her own and stuff but  i think they took it a step too far red  light  yes that means no power a holographic  interface  must be some kind of code resources  that makes one that's two okay i did it  sounds like that didn't if i find more i  should be able to get to that ancient  armor i found  it doesn't even really feel like she's  talking to herself because she's just  weird that way but rather because she's  trying to give constant exposition to  the player  i hate this in movies and i hate it in  video games too  i know this is personal but when i'm  exploring a dark and spoopy hallway i  don't need to constantly hear the  character's thoughts on the situation  it works when you have two characters  who are sharing their thoughts and  observations with each other but on a  solo mission it just comes off as if the  devs are directly trying to tell you  what's going on in case you missed it  so when it comes to link i'm all for not  having him talk during gameplay and as  for cutscenes you can still have a  protagonist who doesn't speak much  but once in a blue moon having them say  at least something would already go a  long way  i mean even silent bob at least says  something once in a while when it's  necessary  just [ __ ] say it already  the sign on the back of the car said  critters  of hollywood you got  again i want to reiterate that this  argument is based on the assumption that  breath of wild sequel will focus more on  the bond between link and zelda if this  is not the case and the story is equally  as minimalistic as breath of wild  a voiced link may not be necessary and  they might want to save it for a future  title one that's more story driven like  skyward sword  so with all this in mind i get the  reasons why people might still be on the  fence about the idea of link speaking  the first one being bad examples set by  past  experiences we all know what i'm talking  about  i can't wait to bomb some dadongos yes  the cheesiness of the 90s sure did put a  dent in people's confidence when it  comes to link being voiced but we have  to keep in mind that none of these  cringe fests were made by nintendo  themselves  and i don't think this should dictate  whether or not nintendo gives it a shot  in their own creations  i think with the release of breath the  wild and even more so age of calamity  nintendo has shown that they are more  than capable of presenting believable  characters with the right voice actor  not always of course which leads into  the next point the fear of choosing the  wrong voice  and yeah this is a fear i can totally  get behind  choosing the right voice actor can make  or break a character we've already seen  a bit of a divide when it comes to  zelda's voice  some think she's fine while others are  less enthusiastic about it let's just  put it that way  if the voice of the main character is  off it could hamper the enjoyment of the  game which also ties into the next  aspect  a general fear of change change can be  difficult and scary  especially with a series that's so  dearly beloved by its fans  we already caught a glimpse of this  during the announcement of the wind  waker back in 2002  safe to say a lot of people were not  happy with the sudden change in art  style  of course this sentiment was quickly  brushed away by most once we saw how  amazing and charming the game actually  was  and i think if there's one fan base that  should be open to change is the zelda  community  we are used to change this is a  franchise that has altered its art  styles gameplay and so many other  aspects so many times since its  inception  the series is one of the most  experimental ones out there and  one of the reasons why it's still going  strong after so many installments  some might argue that it would be weird  if link all of a sudden starts talking  especially if it's the same link from  breath the wild and age of calamity  well i mean dead space did the same  thing and nobody complained about that  in the first game isaac was silent which  was fine because he was alone and  isolated for the majority of his journey  but as the games became more story  driven and more voiced characters got  involved in death space two and three  the devs made the decision to give him a  voice  i think it would be a shame if the idea  of a voiced link is scrapped altogether  just to play it safe how will we know if  it's an improvement or not if they never  try  even if it fails i doubt it would  completely ruin a zelda game altogether  the gameplay will undoubtedly still be  amazing  now you might say zelda doesn't need  voice acting to be good and  i agree the series has been more  incompetent in portraying its story  without the use of spoken word but just  because something is already good  is that really a valid argument not to  try something new or would you miss out  on the opportunity to make it even  better personally i think the latter is  the case here what if it ends up working  extremely well  maybe five years from now we wouldn't  have it any other way breath of the wild  broke so many conventions for the series  some for the worst and some for the  better but the good changes have been so  monumental i honestly can't imagine a 3d  zelda game releasing after this that  doesn't have climbing that doesn't  feature something akin to the paraglider  or the physics and chemistry engine what  if a voice acted link ends up being  another one of these improvements  is it worth playing it safe for the rest  of the series lifespan  just because of the fear that it might  not be an improvement  i will leave that up to you to decide me  i am more open to the idea than ever  before  thank you all for watching a shout out  goes to my amazing patreon supporters  and channel members who make these  videos possible  some of which have recently joined the  cause so give a warm welcome to  johnny peacock glenn clecker zedon  dragmiyer  yoshi 6500 and dare del avid you guys  are the best  thanks a lot for your support that is  all for now this is don  signing off and have a good one  [Music]  you

